  "nameStory": "The Dotted-stalked Suillus gets its name from the tiny granular dots that cover its stem, giving it a sandpaper-like texture. These little bumps are actually glandular dots that become more prominent as the mushroom ages, making them feel rough to the touch like fine sandpaper.",
  "description": "This is a medium-sized bolete mushroom with a slimy, yellowish-brown cap and pores instead of gills underneath. The cap can reach 2-4 inches across and has a sticky, almost greasy feel when wet. What really sets it apart is its dotted stem that feels gritty when you run your finger along it, and the way it often 'weeps' milky droplets from its pore surface when young.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Stem",
      "description": "Covered in tiny granular dots that feel like fine sandpaper when touched - this is the key identifying feature that gives the mushroom its name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap",
      "description": "Yellowish to reddish-brown, very slimy and sticky when wet, feeling almost greasy to the touch like it's been buttered",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pores",
      "description": "Tiny round holes instead of gills, cream to yellow colored, often weeping milky white droplets when young and fresh",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Print",
      "description": "Produces a brown to olive-brown spore print when cap is placed gill-side down on white paper overnight",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smell",
      "description": "Pleasant, mild mushroomy smell with no unpleasant or chemical odors",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in pine forests throughout New England, growing directly from the soil near pine trees where it forms beneficial partnerships with the roots. Look for it in sandy or acidic soils under white pine, red pine, or pitch pine, often appearing in small groups or clusters.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Not typically found, though very early specimens might appear in late spring during warm, wet weather in southern New England.",
    "summer": "Peak season begins in mid to late summer, especially after warm rain showers. Fresh specimens will be actively weeping milky droplets.",
    "fall": "Continues fruiting through early fall, though older specimens may be drier and less slimy. Still identifiable by the granular stem dots.",
    "winter": "Not active, though dried remains might persist on the forest floor as brown, shriveled remnants."
  },
  "funFact": "This mushroom actually 'cries' real tears! When young and fresh, it weeps milky white droplets from its pores - this liquid contains sugars and proteins that attract insects, which then help spread the mushroom's spores on their feet and bodies.",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with the Purple-veiled Slippery Jack, but that species has a purple ring around its stem that this one completely lacks. Remember: 'Granulated has no ring, but it's got that sandpaper sting (texture)!'",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called a 'bolete,' this mushroom belongs to the Slippery Jack group, which are technically separate from true boletes. The 'weeping' behavior that gives it an alternate name is actually a clever reproductive strategy, not a sign of decay.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike its cousin the Purple-veiled Slippery Jack which has a distinctive ring around the stem, the Dotted-stalked Suillus has a completely ringless stem covered in granular dots. It's also much less colorful than the Painted Suillus, lacking any bright reds or oranges, and its stem texture is much grittier than the smooth-stemmed Chicken Fat Mushroom.",
